sensor-w1therm (1.2.0) stable; urgency=medium

  * Documentation: the man page documents exit 20, the location field and
    every field of a reading, and its SEE ALSO names sr(1) rather than a
    sensor-control(1) that does not exist; its header is brought up to this
    version. The README gains a Configuration section and a complete output
    example
  * Remove the MIT LICENSE file. debian/copyright, the README and the source
    header all say GPL-2+, and no other WildlifeSystems package ships a
    LICENSE file; the three that agree are the licence
  * Packaging: debhelper-compat 13 in place of debian/compat 11, as every
    other package; Standards-Version 4.6.1; Homepage and Vcs-Git point at the
    repository under the organisation's real name; the lintian override for
    our "stable" distribution; the systemd unit's Documentation= names
    sensor-w1therm(1), not a sensor-ds18b20(1) that does not exist; the man
    page is installed once, by the Makefile, rather than also by a manpages
    file; .gitignore ignores the binary this package builds rather than one
    called sensor-ds18b20
  * The bash completion offers "temperature", which the driver accepts
  * The location filter test comes from the library, so the three drivers
    agree on it, and the config path is spelt with WS_CONFIG_PATH
  * Warnings and errors go through the library's logging, so they reach
    syslog as well as stderr, and ws_log_init is called so they carry this
    driver's name; they were written to stderr alone, where nothing but the
    per-driver log under /var/log/sensor-control ever saw them
  * "setup" with no sensors present exits 20, the code every other failure
    uses, rather than a bare 1
  * Mock readings declare location "{{node}}", as sensor-onboard's mock
    declares its physical sensors, so every driver's mock has one shape.
    They used to emit location null, which is what an unconfigured sensor
    reports, and so disagreed with the shell driver's mock
  * A sensor that cannot be read is emitted with "value":null and the reason in
    "error", as sensor-dht11 and sensor-bme680 do, rather than dropped with a
    warning on stderr. Dropping it made a failed probe indistinguishable from
    one that was never there, and "sr check", which looks for expected
    sensor_ids in the output, is the tool meant to tell the difference. The
    "No w1_therm sensors detected" warning that also fired when a filter
    excluded every sensor is gone, being wrong in that case
  * Join only the threads that were started. When pthread_create failed the
    sensor was read synchronously and the unstarted thread joined anyway,
    which is undefined behaviour
  * Fail with nothing printed, exit 20, when sc-prototype is unavailable,
    checked once before any conversion is triggered. Every reading used to be
    skipped individually and "[]" printed with exit 0, which claims the node
    has no sensors when it has just found some. This matches sensor-dht11 and
    sensor-bme680
  * sensor_id and sensor_name are now escaped, by libwildlifesystems 1.3.0. A
    quote in either, typed into the config file, ended the JSON string early
    and invalidated every reading this driver emitted
  * Rebuild against libwildlifesystems 1.3.0, so "{{node}}" in a sensor's
    location resolves to the node's own coordinates from /etc/geolocation
    instead of being emitted as an unresolved token
  * Accept "temperature", which list advertises and which was previously
    rejected with exit 20. It reports temperature alone, so naming it asks for
    everything
  * The usage line is generated from the measurement list rather than written by
    hand
  * "mock" is a table of values passed to the library rather than hand-built
    JSON, so it cannot drift from the real output format. It also gains the
    missing-serial fallback the other drivers had, having previously emitted
    "sensor_id":null on a host with no Pi serial
  * The boot config handling moves to libwildlifesystems, which is where it
    belongs: only the overlay line itself was 1-Wire specific. "enable" behaves
    as before and now returns WS_EXIT_* rather than a bare 1 on failure
  * Build the output array with the library's JSON array builder, replacing the
    hand-placed brackets and the is_first comma flag
  * Use the library's WS_UNIT_* constants rather than unit string literals
  * Config parsing moves to the library's ws_config_iter_* and the common
    fields to an embedded ws_sensor_config_base_t, leaving hw_id as the only
    field this driver declares and parses for itself
  * No longer reports a temperature alongside an error. The DS18B20 power-on
    sentinel (85.000) and the insufficient-power sentinel (127.937) were being
    emitted in "value" next to the error naming them, so a consumer reading
    "value" without also checking "error" recorded them as real temperatures.
    Both are now reported as "value":null, with the sentinel quoted in the
    error message where it remains useful for diagnosis
  * Require libwildlifesystems-dev (>= 1.3.0). The library is static, so the
    behaviour above is fixed at build time: a binary built against 1.2.0 would
    satisfy the old pin and silently emit unresolved tokens under the same
    package version

 -- Ed Baker <ed@ebaker.me.uk>  Sat, 12 Sep 2026 14:00:00 +0100

sensor-w1therm (1.1.0) stable; urgency=medium

  * Report device from the 1-Wire family code, so a DS1822 reports "ds1822"
    rather than being conflated with DS18B20
  * Report the sensor's configured location
  * Support "location" in /etc/ws/sensors/w1therm.json
  * Use ws_json_object_end for config object bounds, so a config entry may
    contain nested objects
  * Rebuild against libwildlifesystems 1.2.0
  * Require libwildlifesystems-dev (>= 1.2.0) and sensor-control (>= 1.3.0):
    an older sc-prototype has no device or location field, and the new
    values would be dropped silently rather than failing

 -- Ed Baker <ed@ebaker.me.uk>  Fri, 11 Sep 2026 16:00:00 +0100

